macOS: find libjli in the layout JDK 9 and later use

_load_jli_macos() exists to work around JDK-7131356, where the wrong JVM gets
picked unless libjli is loaded first. It looks for the library in

    <java_home>/jre/lib/jli/libjli
    <java_home>/lib/jli/libjli

which is the layout of JDK 8 and earlier. JDK 9 dropped the separate jre/ tree
and moved the launcher library up one level, so on every modern JDK the file is

    <java_home>/lib/libjli.dylib

and neither candidate exists. The result on a current macOS install is

    Opening /.../Contents/Home/jre/lib/jli/libjli ...
    Opening /.../Contents/Home/lib/jli/libjli ...
    Wasn't able to load JLI

so the workaround silently never applies. It is not fatal -- bdj_open() logs
and carries on to load the JVM -- which is exactly why it has gone unnoticed.

Add the modern directory as a third candidate. It is tried last, so a JDK 8 or
older keeps resolving through the same path it always did, and Apple's Java 6
on 10.5/10.6 (which has no jli launcher at all, see the JavaVM framework patch)
is unaffected either way.

--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/bdj.c
@@ -348,7 +348,10 @@
 {
     const char *java_home = NULL;
     static const char * const jli_dir[]  = {
-        "jre/lib/jli", "lib/jli",
+        /* JDK 8 and earlier, then JDK 9+, which dropped the jre/ tree and
+         * moved libjli from lib/jli/ up into lib/. Tried in that order so the
+         * older layouts keep resolving exactly as before. */
+        "jre/lib/jli", "lib/jli", "lib",
     };
     const unsigned num_jli_dir  = sizeof(jli_dir)  / sizeof(jli_dir[0]);

